@startuml

title ores.shell.workflow Component

set namespaceSeparator ::

namespace ores #F2F2F2 {
    namespace shell #F2F2F2 {
        namespace app #F2F2F2 {
            namespace commands #F2F2F2 {
                namespace workflow #F2F2F2 {
                    class workflow_instance_commands {
                        {static} +register_commands(root_menu : cli::Menu&, session : nats_client&)
                    }
                    class workflow_step_commands {
                        {static} +register_commands(root_menu : cli::Menu&, session : nats_client&)
                    }
                    class workflow_wait_commands {
                        {static} +register_commands(root_menu : cli::Menu&, session : nats_client&)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

ores.shell.api ..> ores.shell.workflow : menu, command feedback, argument parsing
ores.workflow.api ..> ores.shell.workflow : domain, messaging

' The automated pass draws one box per unit and cannot show which of the three a
' person wrote, or why. Two are generated from the workflow entity models, one per
' entity, and each answers its entity's derived verbs. The third is hand-written
' because blocking on a run is not an entity verb: it polls the engine's step query
' until the instance reaches a terminal state, and no entity model can express that.
' Three other command units -- bundles, provision and ORE import -- call its
' wait_for_instance at four sites, so retiring it would break them.

note bottom of "ores::shell::app::commands::workflow::workflow_wait_commands"
Answers `workflow wait <instance_id> [--timeout] [--expect-steps]`,
and exposes wait_for_instance for the command units that dispatch a
workflow and then block on it.

The two units beside it answer the entity protocols, addressed per
row; this one answers the engine, addressed per run. The workflow
component's P04 records the operation model that would generate it,
as iam's *_operations_commands units already are.
end note

